  "tail": "### `thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics` — CERTIFY\n\n**CURRENT TEXT READ:** \"**Claim 4 pads a compact set, not a point.** It says a compact set can always be padded to an open set that is still \"bounded\" in the only sense available here, namely having compact closure. Separating a single point of $X$ from the added point $\\infty$ costs less than that: [[thm-one-point-compactification-properties]] does it at step 1.4 from claim 1 alone, taking a compact neighbourhood of the point and using that it is closed.\"\n\n**ASSESSMENT:** Correct, and correctly cited. I opened `items/thm-one-point-compactification-properties.md` and read its step 1.4 verbatim:\n\n> \"1.4 For the backward half of claim 4 assume $X$ is locally compact and Hausdorff, and let $u \\ne v$ in $X^{*}$. ... If $v = \\infty$ and $u = x \\in X$, then [L7] applied to the neighbourhood $X$ of $x$ gives a compact neighbourhood $C$ of $x$, closed by [L6], and an open $U$ of $X$ with $x \\in U \\subseteq C$; then $U$ and $X^{*} \\setminus C$ are disjoint members of $\\mathcal{T}^{*}$ containing $x$ and $\\infty$.\"\n\nand its L7: \"In a locally compact Hausdorff space every neighbourhood of a point contains a compact neighbourhood of it ([[thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics]], claim 1)\". So step 1.4's only invocation of `thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics` is via L7, which is exactly claim 1 (the neighbourhood-base/shrinking claim), not claim 4. Nowhere else in that item's Facts, Proof, or Remarks is `thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics` cited (only L7, checked by full-text grep of the file). The repaired sentence's description — \"compact neighbourhood of the point, using that it is closed\" — matches step 1.4 exactly: $C$ is produced by claim 1 (via L7) and its closedness comes separately from L6 (Hausdorff ⇒ compact is closed). The claim is scoped correctly: it says the *sibling's point-separation step* uses claim 1, not that claim 4 is globally unused — and it does not overreach in